Alright, I've just finished a series of edits to `src/tui.tsx` to make the UI dynamically adapt to terminal size. I added the `useStdout` hook, tracked `stdout.rows` in state, created a `rowsRef` to hold the current row count, and used that to calculate how many lines of the stream to show (`cap`). I also introduced a comment explaining the dynamic region and wrapped the whole content area in a `<Box flexDirection="column" height={dynRows}>` so that the input bar and status line stay anchored at the bottom.

Now I need to make sure the approval dialog (the `<Approval>` component) is positioned correctly. Right now the JSX for the approval looks like this:

```tsx
{approval && (
  <Approval
    request={approval.request}
    onDecision={(ok, always) => {
      if (always) loadConfig().yolo = true;
      approval.resolve(ok);
      setApproval(null);
    }}
  />
)}
```

Because this block is placed directly after the stream rendering, it will appear immediately below the streamed output, which could push the input bar and status line down or cause the layout to look cramped, especially when the terminal height changes. I want the approval dialog to sit at the very bottom of the UI, just above the input bar, while the stream content occupies the space above it. Ink provides a `<Spacer />` component that expands to fill any remaining space in a flex container. Since I already added `Spacer` to the import list (`import { Box, Spacer, Static, Text, render, useApp, useInput, useStdin, useStdout } from "ink";`), I can use it here.

The plan is to insert a `<Spacer />` right before the conditional rendering of `<Approval>`. This will push the approval component down to the bottom of the column flex container, ensuring the input bar remains at the bottom and the approval dialog appears just above it. I need to keep the existing logic unchanged, just prepend the `<Spacer />` element.

To achieve this, I’ll replace the exact block of code that starts with `{approval && (` and ends with `)}` with a new block that first renders `<Spacer />` and then the same `{approval && ( ... )}` block. I’ll make sure the indentation matches the surrounding code and that I’m not accidentally altering any other part of the file.

Thus, the edit will be:
- `old_string`: the original approval block.
- `new_string`: a `<Spacer />` followed by the unchanged approval block.

This targeted replacement will give the UI the desired layout without affecting any other functionality.

